<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Breda (1667)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1667">1667</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-dutch-war">Second Anglo-Dutch War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a>, signed in July 1667, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-dutch-war">Second Anglo-Dutch War</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. Each side kept what it held: England kept New Netherland, renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> island of Run. France restored Antigua, Montserrat and the English part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a>, and England returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a> to France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-breda-1667.jpg" alt="Treaty of Breda (1667)" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Romeyn de Hooghe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Congress_of_Breda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-dutch-war">Second Anglo-Dutch War</a> grew out of commercial tension. Charles II and his brother James founded the Royal African Company in 1660, challenging the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> enforced a monopoly on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, leaving only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> plantations on Run, which the English reoccupied in late 1664 and the Dutch expelled them from again.</p><p>In August 1664 the English occupied New Netherland, later renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. By late 1666 Charles was short of money, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> economy had largely recovered. Negotiations were slow until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s invasion of the Spanish Netherlands in late May 1667, which the Dutch saw as a graver threat, and the Dutch Medway Raid of June, which deepened English war-weariness.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> was signed on 31 July 1667 and consisted of three treaties between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, along with an Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> commercial agreement. Its principle was uti possidetis, &quot;what you have, you hold&quot;. The Dutch kept Surinam, Fort Cormantin and Run, while the English kept New Netherland, later divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, Massachusetts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> and Delaware.</p><p>A separate treaty amended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navigation-acts">Navigation Acts</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> accepted that free ships make free goods. By the French treaty England returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayenne">Cayenne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>, though the handover was delayed until 1670, and regained Montserrat and Antigua, with the Anglo-French division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> restored.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>By exchanging New Netherland and Run, the treaty removed two major disputes and cleared the way for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-alliance-1668">Triple Alliance</a> of 1668 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In the longer term the English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> came to see <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as a greater threat than each other.</p><p>On 5 August 1667 the States of Holland passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpetual-edict">Perpetual Edict</a>, which abolished the position of stadtholder of Holland, disappointing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orangists">Orangists</a>. Sweden, which had acted as mediator, gained improved terms and a place in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-alliance-1668">Triple Alliance</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> considered the treaty a diplomatic triumph in a period often seen as the high point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-golden-age">Dutch Golden Age</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Breda (1667)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Treaty of Breda</b> of 1667 the Dutch kept Suriname while the English kept New Amsterdam, which they had renamed New York.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The French briefly occupied it in 1666, but the <b>Treaty of Breda</b> in 1667 restored English rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-antigua">Colony of Antigua</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Breda</b> that year confirmed Dutch possession of Suriname in exchange for England keeping New Netherland, renamed New York.</blockquote><a href="/state/willoughbyland">Willoughbyland</a></li></ul></section>
